Z||000004||Rodney||06-23-2000||08:19 AM||||It is not at most auto stores. Usually you need to mail order it. International Auto has pretty good prices on it. 17 oz. spray. #22115 $13.95. 34 oz. refill $17.95. A little goes a long way. If you are serious about cleaning rims without damaging them this is the stuff. I would not be with out it.<P>1-800-726-0555<P>Rodney Dickman||||reg||1
Z||000005||lennyb4||06-23-2000||08:52 AM||||With out a doupt the best wheel cleaner i have ever found is a mixture of four steps.<BR>1.) Spray on wheel cleaner. Let soak and rinse off very well.  (dad does this)<BR>2.) 11 yr old son takes a wash cloth and car wash soap cleans all areas of rim and rinses off.  (dad supervises)<BR>3.) 10 yr old daughter goes over and touches up spots where 11 yr old son missed amd rinses off.  (dad supervises)<BR>4.) 11 yr old son & 10 yr old daughter take two clean wash clothes for the drying process.  (dad supervises)<P>Works out well and your not even tired afterwards :d Its almost like you never touched them yourself. (and its a good excuse to get some ice cream too)||||reg||1
Z||000006||phillip||06-23-2000||10:42 AM||||i have after market painted silver wheels and it might seem strange but what dose best if they are not too dirty to start with is spray furniture polish. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||06-23-2000||08:10 PM||||so far pretty much everything I've used has need of at least some brushing or scrubbing. Old toothbrushes come in handy for this. Old bottle brushes work good on larger openings in the wheels. <P>Full cleaning pretty much means taking off the wheel. You want to do that periodically anyway to prevent brake dust and dirt buildup in the back of the wheel that can unballance the wheel. You should always do this before getting new tires. If you don't the process of mounting the tires can leave loose packs of dirt that come off later and unballance the new rubber.||||reg||1
